Expected elements: present

Every must-show item renders and no must-not-show item appears. The affirmative `Real Aztec mainnet data` banner sits inset with `aztec-mainnet` and the producer's sentences; `Not observable` carries the published pruning reason; Overview shows block, canonicality, finality and cost; internal calls and state changes both say "permanently, not yet"; footer, provenance strip and all three marks are drawn in visible indigo. A full-page hue histogram finds exactly two chromatic families — green and indigo — and zero red or amber pixels, so the must-not-show "error colour or danger tone" is cleanly satisfied. Every text-on-surface pair I measured clears AA: lowest is the nav placeholder #565656 on #dddddd at 5.4:1; body prose runs 9.2–15.5:1. This is a designed light theme, not an inverted dark one.

The colour issues are role assignment, not palette. Green marks three affirmative facts but the adjacent `CANONICAL: Yes` — equally affirmative — is a neutral pill, so no rule governs which facts get chromatic reinforcement. Worse, the page's headline state is its palest chip: `Not observable` is white-on-white with a hairline while routine metadata shouts green, inverting emphasis against meaning. Containers use three unmapped treatments, and the Raw panel's fill equals the canvas exactly.

Highest-priority fixes: give `Not observable` a distinct non-alarming strong-neutral tone, and settle one rule for when an affirmative value earns green.

Rating: 7/10

